From: Thorch contributors
Subject: [PATCH] input: qcom-hv-haptics: restore the rail per play and finish the flag fix

Two defects left the upload-once/play-many pattern working only for the
first tap, and one left chip_is_playing able to stick.

The haptics rail is dropped on every stop. haptics_stop_constant_effect_play()
ends each play with:

	rc = haptics_enable_hpwr_vreg(chip, false);

but the rail is only brought back up in haptics_upload_effect(). A client
that uploads one effect when it opens the device and then plays it per
keypress therefore runs every play after the first with the rail down.
That is the strong/weak alternation seen on the AYN Thor's on-screen
keyboard: the first tap has the rail because the upload enabled it, the
per-play stop tears it down, and the next tap is attenuated until
something else restores it. The re-armed stop timer from 0227 does not
help here, it only bounds how long the motor runs.

Enable the rail from haptics_playback() before starting, mirroring what
the upload path already does. haptics_enable_hpwr_vreg() returns
immediately when the rail is already in the requested state, so this is
free for clients that keep it up.

Because play never started on the enable_play() failure path, stop_work
is never armed there, so nothing would turn the rail back off. Drop it
again explicitly before returning, or a play that keeps failing leaves
the rail energized until an erase or a teardown path happens to run.

Second, 0226 routed the three failure paths in haptics_enable_play()
through out_not_playing but missed the boost vreg one, which still did a
plain return. chip_is_playing stays true there, and since
haptics_playback() only starts the motor when it is false, every later
play silently returns 0. Route it through the label as well.

Third, constant_replay_ms is now stale after an erase. haptics_erase()
cancels stop_work and stops playback, but left the recorded replay length
behind, so a later playback() could arm a stop timer sized for an effect
that no longer exists. Clear it alongside chip_effect_loaded.

Fixes: 0226-input-qcom-haptics-arm-stop-per-ff-constant-play.patch

---
 drivers/input/misc/qcom-hv-haptics.c | 26 ++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/input/misc/qcom-hv-haptics.c
+++ b/drivers/input/misc/qcom-hv-haptics.c
@@ -1776,7 +1776,7 @@
 			if (rc < 0) {
 				dev_err(chip->dev, "Keep boost vreg on failed, rc=%d\n",
 						rc);
-				return rc;
+				goto out_not_playing;
 			}
 		} else {
 			hrtimer_start(&chip->hbst_off_timer,
@@ -2872,9 +2872,31 @@
 		if (!chip->chip_effect_loaded)
 			dev_warn(chip->dev, "Trying to playback while no effect is loaded!\n");
 
+		/*
+		 * Re-enable the haptics rail per play, not just per upload.
+		 * haptics_stop_constant_effect_play() drops the rail every
+		 * time a play ends, so a client that uploads once and plays
+		 * many times runs every play after the first with the rail
+		 * down. haptics_enable_hpwr_vreg() is a no-op when the rail
+		 * is already in the requested state.
+		 */
+		rc = haptics_enable_hpwr_vreg(chip, true);
+		if (rc < 0) {
+			dev_err(chip->dev, "enable hpwr_vreg for play failed, rc=%d\n",
+					rc);
+			return rc;
+		}
+
 		rc = haptics_enable_play(chip, true);
-		if (rc < 0)
+		if (rc < 0) {
+			/*
+			 * Play never started, so stop_work was never armed.
+			 * Drop the rail again here or it stays energized until
+			 * an erase or a teardown path happens to run.
+			 */
+			haptics_enable_hpwr_vreg(chip, false);
 			return rc;
+		}
 
 		/*
 		 * Arm the stop here, once playback has actually started, so
@@ -2939,6 +2961,7 @@
 	}
 
 	chip->chip_effect_loaded = false;
+	chip->constant_replay_ms = 0;
 
 	mutex_unlock(&play->lock);
 
